WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE
Invented sample captions for the nightshift demo. Creator: Lena Moreau (fictional).

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
In ten minutes I want to explain retrieval augmented generation, or RAG, without any hype.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
The idea is simple. Instead of hoping the model remembers your documents, you retrieve the relevant pieces and put them in the prompt.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Step one is chunking. Split your documents into pieces of a few hundred words, with a small overlap so sentences are not cut in half.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Step two is embeddings. Each chunk becomes a vector, and you store the vectors in a database. I use Supabase with the vector extension, but Pinecone works just as well.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Step three is retrieval. When a question comes in, you embed the question, find the five closest chunks, and paste them into the prompt with the question.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:47.000
The trick is to also store the title and the section heading with every chunk, because retrieval quality jumps when the chunk knows where it came from.

7
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:56.000
For the prompt itself I use: "Answer the question using only the context below. If the context does not contain the answer, say you do not know and list what is missing."

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.000
That last sentence matters. It cuts made-up answers dramatically, and it tells you which documents you should add to the knowledge base.

9
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:14.000
You can build all of this with LangChain in an afternoon, and I wrote most of my glue code in Cursor agent mode, which handled the boring parts really well.

10
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.000
A common mistake is chunks that are too large. With huge chunks the retrieval step returns vague matches and the answer quality drops.

11
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Another pattern I like is hybrid search, which combines keyword search with vector search. Keyword search catches exact product names and error codes that embeddings often miss.

12
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:40.000
If you want a side project idea, someone should build a RAG starter kit for internal documentation of small software teams, with a one-click import from a wiki.

13
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Teams of 10 to 50 developers waste hours every week searching old docs, and they would happily pay for something that just works.

14
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
That is RAG in ten minutes. Start with a small set of documents, measure the answers, and only then scale up.
